ollect holds raw pointer tokens_ — lifetime fine (same scope).\\n- decode's `counted` guard \\\"all records ≥ 1 byte\\\" — true for these record shapes ✓.\\n\\nVerdict: SIGNED OFF (G1 is low, recommend fix in next round; not blocking? The load-time NPE only bites on hypothetical nil shim, and shiva evidence contradicts nil on Linux. Make it SIGNED OFF with G1 noted for J0c round 3 / Linux run.) Hmm — \\\"SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES\\\" would demand a fix before merge. Given Linux not run and shim behavior empirically fine on shiva, I'll go SIGNED OFF with recommendation. Actually adversarial stance: this is production-track source (src/jolt), and repo's own probe documents distrust of os.name on jolt. A load-time crash on Linux would be discovered immediately in shiva run and os.name=\\\"Linux\\\" is proven in shiva. So risk is minimal → SIGNED OFF with G1 as recommendation.\\n\\nWord cap 600. Structure tight.\\n\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 10:12:02 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 17:12:02 +07\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\\n# Review: J0c round 2 (corpus parity, cst.jolt adapter, export entry)\\nRole: Reviewer (read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\\n\\n## Verdict: SIGNED OFF\\n\\nThe 5 divergences are reported, isolated and left for the Architect per instruction; the work itself is correct and honest.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n| id | sev | file:line | evidence | correction |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| G1 | low | src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:38-49 | Platform is derived at load from `System/getProperty \\\"os.name\\\"`; the J0b probe's own comment (udp_probe.clj:13-14) says jolt's shim \\\"can answer nil\\\" on Linux, and `(str/lower-case nil)` would NPE the namespace load. shiva's J0b log shows `os.name= Linux`, so it works empirically, but the distrust is documented in-repo. | Use `jolt.socket.native/os` (as the J0b probe does) or nil-guard, before the Linux J0c run. |\\n| G2 | info | jolt_parser.cpp:398-399 | `jolt_parser_layout_version` still answers 1 (the JPT1 version); JPX1's version travels only inside its buffer. The adapter checks it there, so no gap today. | Give the export layout its own version entry when layouts evolve. |\\n| G3 | info | src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub/ | Stand-in root sits under `src/dev`; it cannot resolve on the JVM (namespace maps from the subdirectory, not `src/dev`), but scanners (kondo, affected.clj) may pick it up. Author already flags this. | Keep the flag; consider `build/` or a `jolt-` prefix convention if it lingers past J0c. |\\n\\n## Checks\\n\\n- (a) Oracle integrity: `jolt_j0c_oracle.clj` requires only JVM-path namespaces (`yang.antlr.cst`, `yang.python.antlr.parser`, `yang.python.antlr.repl-probe`) and runs under `clojure -M:dev`; no jolt input exists in the generator, and the fixture header forbids cross-host regeneration. Ordering is credible: determinism re-run after all jolt runs gives the identical SHA-256 (r2-oracle-rerun.log), and the parity script itself asserts `:host :jvm`, grammar-id equality and corpus/oracle id+source alignment (jolt_j0c_parity.clj:65-71). No tuning: the corpus deliberately includes the entries that expose the known round-1 helper divergence (`blank-lines`, `malformed-unexpected-indent/dedent`), and divergences were reported rather than removed. §7.8 coverage complete (I counted 51 entries: arithmetic incl. int64-max, nested indent/dedent + double-dedent-at-EOF, empty/EOF, non-ASCII + astral, malformed ×9, incomplete multiline ×4, triple quotes ×4, open brackets ×4, early-error-under-bracket ×3) and W8 respected: no floats, nothing beyond int64 (`9223372036854775807 - -9223372036854775807` stays in range).\\n- (b) `jolt_parser_export` keeps the round-1 containment exactly: same -1/-2/-3/-4 statuses, shared `noexcept` nonallocating `message()`, shared fault seam; listener-side `rethrow_exception` is wrapped in its own catch-all inside the outer try. cst.jolt: buffer copied then freed in `finally` (call-export), no pointer escapes; decode is fully bounds-checked (`need` before every read, negative lengths refused), validates magic/layout/exact-consumption/status-vs-errors/record-kinds, and `counted`'s ≥1-byte-per-record bound prevents count-driven overallocation; `children-of` enforces one root consuming all records with rule indexes in-table. Nonzero wrapper status throws before any packet (parity modes 1-3 + clean re-parse: 4 PASS). Shared export/packet code loaded unchanged (load-closure assertion :47-50).\\n- (c) First-failure evidence is solid: per-entry RECORD lines show buffered-at-report < buffered-after-parse (lazy fill, as Java), the JVM rule matches 51/51 oracle results, and the JS/Dart alternative was computed for every entry and demonstrably disagrees on `\\\"f(1,\\\\n\\\"` where the oracle says `:end`. Empirical, not assumed.\\n- (d) The stand-in supplies only `make-worker` (delegating to the real adapter) and `grammar-id`; the parity run proves the tracked `parser.cljc` did NOT load (`rule-names` unresolved) and cross-reads the grammar id from the tracked source. The probe code exercised is the tracked `repl_probe.cljc`, so no shared-code gap is hidden; the missing `:jolt` branch of the real `parser.cljc` is disclosed as later work.\\n- (e) Revert verified: manifest `:cpp :generated` digests byte-identical to round 1; the on-disk generated `Python3LexerBase.cpp` shows only the manifest's `positionedToken` patch and neither experiment line (`getLine(), getCharPositionInLine()` in `commonToken`, `dedent->setLine`); digest-checked regeneration exit 0. Dylib hash `1e465456…` rests on the report.\\n- (f) Honesty: extensive NOT-done list (worker caches/§7.4, two-worker and thread tests, collect-safety, §7.6 recursion budget, load-time library failure vs J5, profile constructors ignored, grammar-id not cross-checked against the dylib, NUL/invalid-UTF-8, sanitizers, exported-symbols list, lowering, Linux). Stopped at reporting per Q3; no oracle edit, no fallback, no kept patch. Totals in the log (46/51 packets, 51/51 first-failure, 40/40 probes, `SUMMARY 5 diverged, 0 failed`) match the report.\\n\\n## Could not verify\\n\\n- Digests/hashes myself (no shell): corpus `08d3fba…`, oracle `5aee6d1…`, dylib `1e465456…`, and the `cmp` against the saved helper copy.\\n- Timestamps/order of oracle vs first jolt run beyond log names and the determinism re-run.\\n- The JVM `yang.antlr.cst` first-failure rule source (parity is empirical via 51/51).\\n- Linux behaviour of cst.jolt (G1) and all shiva runs for this round (none claimed).\"}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1792491409196,\"messageId\":\"4d1b02e0-23c4-40c7-9dcc-89bf1b79528c\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 11:47:32 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 18:47:32 +07\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\\n# Task: review J0c round 3 (E1 standard, C++ helper patch, layout v2)\\nRole: Reviewer (read-only)\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max | Assigned: 2026-10-20 18:47:32 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: owner routing \\\"cmd for review\\\"; author claude-opus-5-5\\n\\nREAD-ONLY review of the UNCOMMITTED round-3 change in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c (branch jolt-j0c, HEAD 29986f9a): modified antlr/python3/manifest.edn, native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp, src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj, src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt. Author report: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792491448000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r3.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md. The Architect's ruling it implements: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792491122000-architect-j0c-error-parity.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md (equivalence E1; helper patch required). The orchestrator re-ran: oracle fixtures unchanged vs HEAD (sha256 5aee6d10), bb build:jolt-native exit 0, parity exit 0 \\\"SUMMARY ok\\\", round-1 probe 11 PASS, smoke exit 0.\\nCheck: (a) is the E1 projection in the harness FAITHFUL to the ruling (strict for ok packets, first-failure, probe answers; for error packets: validate!, >=1 error, first error kind+span equal, line/column equal for real tokens and equal to pinned per-host constants for lexer-made tokens, non-empty message; message text and errors after the first exempt)? Could the harness pass while the packets are wrong? Are the 7 deliberately-broken self-test packets a real test of the checker (does it refuse them for the right reasons)? Are the 9 pinned entries honest (pins equal the JVM values, each with a reason) or is the pin set tuned to pass; the ruling said pins live in the fixture but the brief put them in the harness: acceptable? (b) the C++ helper patch in the manifest: digest-pinned, same mechanism as Java/JS/Dart, fails closed; is the DEDENT guard (missing previous token) sound and disclosed; do Java/JS/Dart :generated digests and manifest entries stay byte-identical to base (verify against git); (c) the native layout v2 (token type added to error records): backward compatibility of the round-1 entry point and round-2 JPX1 buffer, validation in cst.jolt, version checks, exception containment and fault seam unchanged; (d) G1: cst.jolt platform handling; (e) does the report match the logs; is anything forbidden touched; is the report honest about what is NOT done (Linux unrun, G1 untested on Linux, section 7.8 wording unconfirmed). Under 600 words, final response only, header fields, verdict SIGNED OFF / SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES / NOT SIGNED OFF, findings table (id, severity, file:line, evidence, correction), what you could not verify.\"}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"user\",\"createdAt\":1792496859053,\"messageId\":\"c90fc5dc-0656-44e0-a339-c9a2a259d676\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Read the report, the architect's ruling, and the four modified files. Then verify.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I'll read the round-3 report and th