laude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-138-                 (fn []\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-139-                   {:line (u32), :column (u32), :message (text),\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:140:                    :span-kind (u8), :a (i32), :b (i32), :type (i32)}))\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-141-        failure {:kind (u8), :no-viable (u8), :index (i32), :buffered (u32)}\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:142:        tokens (counted (fn [] {:type (i32), :index (i32), :start (i32),\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-143-                                :stop (i32)}))]\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-144-    (when-not (= @pos length)\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-145-      (refuse \\\"trailing bytes\\\" {:consumed @pos, :length length}))\\n--\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-200-  {:token? (fn [i] (= :token (:kind (nth nodes i)))),\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-201-   :token (fn [i]\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-202-            (let [{:keys [type start stop text]} (nth nodes i)]\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:203:              {:type-name (type-name records type),\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-204-               :start start,\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-205-               :stop stop,\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-206-               :text text})),\\n--\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-258-   the first error failed on an end-of-input token, else `:early`\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-259-   (`export/failure-kind` decides).\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-260-\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:261:   The failing token follows the JVM rule: the offending token of a\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-262-   mismatch, and for a no-viable-alternative error the last token buffered\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-263-   when it was reported, since the C++ runtime's BufferedTokenStream fills\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-264-   lazily as the Java one does (observed against the JVM oracle in J0c round\"}]},{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_2cb76ea87b1e4d1ab1074e34\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:21:                   \\\"Dart/Python3ParserBase.dart\\\" {:blake3 \\\"e4c4693a640e53b971d37088809c74539cc0f1dda7d9c6a3cdd9beb7ce40a87d\\\"},\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:103:               \\\"855f47de669d2f042839efded7e2468e102091b55097f3e403a24a0e3485b860\\\",\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:105:               \\\"4724a9da8856cf081c9f31c7a6974458829124f77bb19a7fd7790b80a567c206\\\",\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:107:               \\\"37f27bdb7036ae9907b231aade8401ab50f81b4061db16c926ba70fe12067972\\\",\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:109:               \\\"a569e60fdf199334f96147bf1d4b1ed028a4b99f70a9f85c88eb3871a47b7a58\\\"}},\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:127:               \\\"a29f3f8cceb6ec2b76185bf563d0e186bfb3d2f39cad2129c57dc31603bdb23b\\\",\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:129:               \\\"e3b11f882d64f64d843d8ab10a133a09cb076452dc3397a6c1fc708c3c026bd5\\\",\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:131:               \\\"fa00271187b2ba976be61c81acd3309b9ea8d19d458ce4a13a5ba1ed971ccf70\\\",\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:133:               \\\"e4c4693a640e53b971d37088809c74539cc0f1dda7d9c6a3cdd9beb7ce40a87d\\\"}}\"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"10422e07-bed5-4d49-b8ad-810ba98736ef\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The JS/Dart digests are identical to the base ✓. The Java digest is identical ✓ (confirmed earlier). cst.jolt: layout must be 2 (:123), decode `:type` with error (:140), platform nil guard (:37) — os is nil → \\\"\\\" → not \\\"mac\\\" → linux; arch is nil → x86_64. G1 has been addressed (with the caveat that nil is read as linux; no problem on macOS hosts where os.name is set; on a nil-shim Linux host it becomes linux, which is correct as per the udp_probe comment). The rationale is also documented. Good.\\n\\nNext is the parity harness — an important adversarial target. Going to read all of jolt_j0c_parity.clj.\\n\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Now the E1 harness itself, the key adversarial target.\\n\\n\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_f21af1df565c42a58da3362e\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj\"}}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1792496954432,\"messageId\":\"de6e5f0b-8381-4f4e-bdfa-c7ae5e845b7f\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_f21af1df565c42a58da3362e\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read 1/1 file, 339 lines\\n1: ;; J0c rounds 2-3: the jolt yang.antlr.cst adapter against the JVM oracle\\n2: ;; (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §7.8), under the Architect's\\n3: ;; equivalence E1 (collab/1792491122000-architect-j0c-error-parity...).\\n4: ;;\\n5: ;;   . build/tools/jolt-env.sh\\n6: ;;   \\\"$JOLT_HOME\\\"/bin/jolt -Srepro \\\\\\n7: ;;     -Sdeps '{:aliases {:j0c-probe-stub {:extra-paths [\\\"src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub\\\"]}}}' \\\\\\n8: ;;     -M:jolt:j0c-probe-stub src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj\\n9: ;;\\n10: ;; For every jolt_j0c_corpus.edn entry, the packet from the jolt adapter\\n11: ;; (src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt, the ANTLR4 C++ parser marshalled into the\\n12: ;; shared yang.antlr.export) passes packet/validate! (source-aware) and meets\\n13: ;; E1 against the JVM oracle:\\n14: ;;\\n15: ;;   strict   every field of an ok packet; outcome, version, unit, grammar\\n16: ;;            and completeness of an error packet; cst/first-failure; every\\n17: ;;            probe answer and its cst/first-failure call sequence\\n18: ;;   errors   at least one; the FIRST error's :kind and :span equal the\\n19: ;;            JVM's; its :line/:column equal the JVM's when the offending\\n20: ;;            token is a real source token, and equal the pinned jolt\\n21: ;;            constant below when it is helper-fabricated (INDENT, DEDENT,\\n22: ;;            EOF, a zero-width NEWLINE); its :message is a non-empty string\\n23: ;;   exempt   message text and expecting-sets, and errors after the first\\n24: ;;\\n25: ;; Strict packet equality is still reported, as RECORD strict-diff lines, so\\n26: ;; nothing E1 exempts is hidden. For every probe text,\\n27: ;; yang.python.antlr.repl-probe/probe runs under jolt and must answer as on the\\n28: ;; JVM, making the same cst/first-failure calls with the same results.\\n29: ;;\\n30: ;; repl-probe requires yang.python.antlr.parser, whose :clj branch imports the\\n31: ;; generated Java recognizers; the command above therefore puts a stand-in\\n32: ;; yang.python.antlr.parser (src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub, holding only\\n33: ;; `make-worker`, the probe's one use of it, and the grammar id) on a root\\n34: ;; before the :jolt roots. The probe's own code is the tracked source.\\n35: ;;\\n36: ;; Prints PASS/DIVERGES per entry with a minimal diff, RECORD lines for the\\n37: ;; observations, and exits nonzero on any divergence or failure.\\n38: (ns jolt-j0c-parity\\n39:   (:require\\n40:     [clojure.edn :as edn]\\n41:     [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\\n42: \\n43: \\n44: (def ^:private before (set (loaded-libs)))\\n45: \\n46: \\n47: (require '[yang.antlr.cst :as cst] '[yang.antlr.export :as export]\\n48:          '[yang.antlr.packet :as packet])\\n49: \\n50: \\n51: (def ^:private added (sort (remove before (loaded-libs))))\\n52: \\n53: (def corpus-path \\\"test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn\\\")\\n54: (def oracle-path \\\"test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_oracle.edn\\\")\\n55: \\n56: (def diverged (atom []))\\n57: (def failed (atom 0))\\n58: \\n59: \\n60: (defn- check\\n61:   [label ok? detail]\\n62:   (when-not ok? (swap! failed inc))\\n63:   (println (if ok? \\\"PASS\\\" \\\"FAIL\\\") label (pr-str detail)))\\n64: \\n65: \\n66: (check \\\"yang.antlr.cst resolves to the jolt adapter (export-records exists) and loads only export/packet beside it\\\"\\n67:        (and (some? (resolve 'yang.antlr.cst/export-records))\\n68:             (= '(yang.antlr.cst yang.antlr.export yang.antlr.packet) added))\\n69:        added)\\n70: \\n71: \\n72: ;; The grammar id the JVM profile carries, read from its one definition.\\n73: (def grammar-id\\n74:   (let [src (slurp \\\"src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc\\\")\\n75:         [_ s] (re-find #\\\"\\\\(def grammar-id\\\\s+\\\\\\\"[^\\\\\\\"]*\\\\\\\"\\\\s+(\\\\\\\"[^\\\\\\\"]*\\\\\\\")\\\\)\\\" src)]\\n76:     (edn/read-string s)))\\n77: \\n78: \\n79: (def profile {:grammar-id grammar-id})\\n80: (def corpus (edn/read-string (slurp corpus-path)))\\n81: (def oracle (edn/read-string (slurp oracle-path)))\\n82: \\n83: \\n84: (check \\\"oracle is the JVM's, for this grammar id, and covers the corpus in order\\\"\\n85:        (and (= :jvm (:host oracle))\\n86:             (= grammar-id (:grammar-id oracle))\\n87:             (= (mapv (juxt :id :source) (:entries corpus))\\n88:                (mapv (juxt :id :source) (:entries oracle)))\\n89:             (= (:probe corpus) (mapv :text (:probe oracle))))\\n90:        {:host (:host oracle), :grammar-id grammar-id})\\n91: \\n92: \\n93: ;; -- minimal diffs -------------------------------------------------------------\\n94: \\n95: (defn- map-diff\\n96:   [a b]\\n97:   (into {}\\n98:         (keep (fn [k]\\n99:                 (when (not= (get a k) (get b k))\\n100:                   [k {:jvm (get a k), :jolt (get b k)}])))\\n101:         (distinct (concat (keys a) (keys b)))))\\n102: \\n103: \\n104: (defn- seq-diff\\n105:   \\\"Index-wise differences of two record vectors, at most `limit` of them.\\\"\\n106:   [xs ys limit]\\n107:   (let [n (max (count xs) (count ys))]\\n108:     (->> (range n)\\n109:          (keep (fn [i]\\n110:                  (let [x (get xs i) y (get ys i)]\\n111:                    (when (not= x y)\\n112:                      [i (if (and (map? x) (map? y)) (map-diff x y) {:jvm x, :jolt y})]))))\\n113:          (take limit)\\n114:          vec)))\\n115: \\n116: \\n117: (defn- packet-diff\\n118:   [jvm jolt]\\n119:   (let [top (dissoc (map-diff (dissoc jvm :yang.cst/nodes :yang.cst/errors)\\n120:                               (dissoc jolt :yang.cst/nodes :yang.cst/errors)))]\\n121:     (cond-> {}\\n122:       (seq top) (assoc :fields top)\\n123:       (not= (:yang.cst/nodes jvm) (:yang.cst/nodes jolt))\\n124:       (assoc :nodes {:count [(count (:yang.cst/nodes jvm)) (count (:yang.cst/nodes jolt))],\\n125:                      :first (seq-diff (vec (:yang.cst/nodes jvm))\\n126:                                       (vec (:yang.cst/nodes jolt)) 4)})\\n127:       (not= (:yang.cst/errors jvm) (:yang.cst/errors jolt))\\n128:       (assoc :errors (seq-diff (vec (:yang.cst/errors jvm))\\n129:                                (vec (:yang.cst/errors jolt)) 8)))))\\n130: \\n131: \\n132: ;; -- the corpus ----------------------------------------------------------------\\n133: \\n134: (defn- alternative-failure\\n135:   \\\"The JS/Dart rule (the listener's offending token even for no-viable-alt),\\n136:    as an observation of the C++ token buffer.\\\"\\n137:   [source]\\n138:   (let [{:keys [failure tokens]} (cst/export-records source)\\n139:         {:keys [kind index]} failure]\\n140:     (export/failure-kind\\n141:       (case kind 0 nil 1 :lexical 2 index)\\n142:       (keep (fn [{:keys [type index start stop]}]\\n143:               (when (not= -1 type) {:index index, :start start, :stop stop}))\\n144:             tokens))))\\n145: \\n146: \\n147: (def helper-pins\\n148:   \\\"The jolt host's [line column] for each entry whose first error is on a\\n149:    helper-fabricated token (macOS arm64, the round-3 :cpp helper patch). They\\n150:    equal the JVM's because that patch gives the C++ helper the Java helper's\\n151:    coordinates (lexer line/column for INDENT and EOF, last token's line for\\n152:    DEDENT); a drift in any of them fails the run.\\\"\\n153:   {:blank-lines [3 2],                  ; INDENT on line 3 after its spaces\\n154:    :missing-final-newline [1 5],        ; EOF at the end of line 1\\n155:    :malformed-unexpected-indent [1 2],  ; INDENT on line 1 after its spaces\\n156:    :malformed-unexpected-dedent [2 0],  ; DEDENT takes the last token's line\\n157:    :incomplete-def-header [2 0],        ; EOF at the start of line 2\\n158:    :incomplete-decorator [2 0],         ; EOF at the start of line 2\\n159:    :incomplete-continuation [2 0],      ; EOF at the start of line 2\\n160:    :bracket-list-open [3 0],            ; EOF at the start of line 3\\n161:    :bracket-dict-open [2 0]})           ; EOF at the start of line 2\\n162: \\n163: \\n164: (defn- helper-token?\\n165:   \\\"Whether the first error's offending token was fabricated by the lexer\\n166:    helper rather than read from the source.\\\"\\n167:   [records]\\n168:   (let [{:keys [type a b]} (first (:errors records))\\n169:         tn (cond (= -1 type) \\\"EOF\\\"\\n170:                  (< -1 type (count (:symbolic records))) ((:symbolic records) type))]\\n171:     (boolean (or (#{\\\"INDENT\\\" \\\"DEDENT\\\" \\\"EOF\\\"} tn)\\n172:                  (and (= \\\"NEWLINE\\\" tn) (< b a))))))\\n173: \\n174: \\n175: (defn- e1-violations\\n176:   \\\"E1 for one entry; [] when it holds.\\\"\\n177:   [id source jvm jolt records]\\n178:   (let [bad (volatile! [])\\n179:         note! (fn [why detail] (vswap! bad conj [why detail]))]\\n180:     (when-let [v (try (packet/validate! jolt {:source source}) nil\\n181:                       (catch Throwable e (ex-data e)))]\\n182:       (note! :validate! v))\\n183:     (if (= :yang.cst/ok (:yang.cst/outcome jvm))\\n184:       (when-not (= jvm jolt) (note! :ok-packet-not-equal (packet-diff jvm jolt)))\\n185:       (let [base (fn [p] (dissoc p :yang.cst/errors))\\n186:             [je] (:yang.cst/errors jvm)\\n187:             [ke] (:yang.cst/errors jolt)\\n188:             helper? (helper-token? records)\\n189:             pin (get helper-pins id)]\\n190:         (when-not (= (base jvm) (base jolt))\\n191:           (note! :packet-fields (map-diff (base jvm) (base jolt))))\\n192:         (when-not (seq (:yang.cst/errors jolt)) (note! :no-errors nil))\\n193:         (when-not (= (:kind je) (:kind ke)) (note! :first-kind [(:kind je) (:kind ke)]))\\n194:         (when-not (= (:span je) (:span ke)) (note! :first-span [(:span je) (:span ke)]))\\n195:         (when-not (and (string? (:message ke)) (seq (:message ke)))\\n196:           (note! :first-message (:message ke)))\\n197:         (cond\\n198:           (and helper? (nil? pin)) (note! :helper-token-unpinned [(:line ke) (:column ke)])\\n199:           (and (not helper?) pin) (note! :pin-on-a-real-token pin)\\n200:           helper? (when-not (= pin [(:line ke) (:column ke)])\\n201:                     (note! :helper-pin-drift {:pin pin, :jolt [(:line ke) (:column ke)]}))\\n202:           :else (when-not (= [(:line je) (:column je)] [(:line ke) (:column ke)])\\n203:                   (note! :first-line-column {:jvm [(:line je) (:column je)],\\n204:                                              :jolt [(:line ke) (:column ke)]})))))\\n205:     @bad))\\n206: \\n207: \\n208: (def results (atom []))\\n209: \\n210: \\n211: (doseq [{:keys [id source packet first-failure]} (:entries oracle)]\\n212:   (let [worker (cst/make-worker profile)\\n213:         jolt-packet (try (cst/parse-unit worker [:unit 0] source)\\n214:                          (catch Throwable e {:thrown (ex-message e), :data (ex-data e)}))\\n215:         jolt-ff (try (cst/first-failure worker source)\\n216:                      (catch Throwable e {:thrown (ex-message e)}))\\n217:         records (cst/export-records source)\\n218:         strict (= packet jolt-packet)\\n219:         e1 (if (:thrown jolt-packet)\\n220:              [[:thrown jolt-packet]]\\n221:              (e1-violations id source packet jolt-packet records))\\n222:         ff-ok (= first-failure jolt-ff)\\n223:         ok (and (empty? e1) ff-ok)\\n224:         alt (alternative-failure source)]\\n225:     (swap! results conj {:id id, :strict strict, :e1 (empty? e1), :first-failure ff-ok})\\n226:     (when-not ok (swap! diverged conj id))\\n227:     (println (if ok \\\"PASS\\\" \\\"FAIL-E1\\\") id\\n228:              (pr-str (cond-> {:outcome (:yang.cst/outcome packet),\\n229:                               :nodes (count (:yang.cst/nodes packet)),\\n230:                               :first-failure first-failure,\\n231:                               :strict strict}\\n232:                        (seq e1) (assoc :e1-violations e1)\\n233:                        (not ff-ok) (assoc :jolt-first-failure jolt-ff))))\\n234:     (when-not (or strict (:thrown jolt-packet))\\n235:       (println \\\"RECORD strict-diff\\\" id (pr-str (packet-diff packet jolt-packet))))\\n236:     (println \\\"RECORD token-buffer\\\" id\\n237:              (pr-str {:first-error (:failure records),\\n238:                       :buffered-after (count (:tokens records)),\\n239:                       :offending-token-rule alt,\\n240:                       :rules-agree (= alt jolt-ff)}))))\\n241: \\n242: \\n243: (check \\\"every helper pin names a corpus entry\\\"\\n244:        (every? (set (map :id (:entries oracle))) (keys helper-pins))\\n245:        (remove (set (map :id (:entries oracle))) (keys helper-pins)))\\n246: \\n247: \\n248: ;; -- E1 refuses what it must (the checker is not vacuous) ------------------------\\n249: \\n250: (let [entry (fn [id] (first (filter #(= id (:id %)) (:entries oracle))))\\n251:       run (fn [id f]\\n252:             (let [{:keys [source packet]} (entry id)\\n253:                   jolt (f (cst/parse-unit (cst/make-worker profile) [:unit 0] source))]\\n254:               (set (map first (e1-violations id source packet jolt\\n255:                                              (cst/export-records source))))))\\n256:       first-error (fn [k f] #(update-in % [:yang.cst/errors 0 k] f))]\\n257:   (doseq [[label id f expect]\\n258:           [[\\\"an INDENT at line 0 (the round-2 C++ helper)\\\" :malformed-unexpected-indent\\n259:             (first-error :line (constantly 0)) #{:validate! :helper-pin-drift}]\\n260:            [\\\"a helper-token column off its pin\\\" :incomplete-decorator\\n261:             (first-error :column inc) #{:helper-pin-drift}]\\n262:            [\\\"a real-token line off the JVM's\\\" :malformed-def\\n263:             (first-error :line inc) #{:first-line-column}]\\n264:            [\\\"a first-error span off the JVM's\\\" :malformed-def\\n265:             (first-error :span (fn [[a b]] [a (inc b)])) #{:first-span}]\\n266:            [\\\"an empty message\\\" :malformed-def\\n267:             (first-error :message (constantly \\\"\\\")) #{:first-message}]\\n268:            [\\\"no errors\\\" :malformed-def\\n269:             #(assoc % :yang.cst/errors []) #{:no-errors}]\\n270:            [\\\"an ok packet with one node changed\\\" :arith-add\\n271:             #(assoc-in % [:yang.cst/nodes 1 :rule] \\\"expr\\\") #{:ok-packet-not-equal}]]]\\n272:     (let [got (run id f)]\\n273:       (check (str \\\"[E1 self-test] refuses \\\" label)\\n274:              (every? got expect)\\n275:              got))))\\n276: \\n277: \\n278: ;; -- a failed native parse never yields a packet --------------------------------\\n279: \\n280: (ffi/defcfn c-inject-fault \\\"jolt_parser_inject_fault\\\" [:int] :int)\\n281: \\n282: \\n283: (doseq [mode [1 2 3]]\\n284:   (c-inject-fault mode)\\n285:   (let [r (try (cst/parse-unit (cst/make-worker profile) [:unit 0] \\\"1 + 2\\\\n\\\")\\n286:                (catch Throwable e {:thrown (ex-data e)})\\n287:                (finally (c-inject-fault 0)))]\\n288:     (check (str \\\"[injected mode \\\" mode \\\"] parse-unit throws the wrapper status, no packet\\\")\\n289:            (and (contains? r :thrown) (#{-2 -3} (get-in r [:thrown :status])))\\n290:            r)))\\n291: \\n292: \\n293: (check \\\"after the injected failures parse-unit answers the oracle packet again\\\"\\n294:        (= (:packet (first (:entries oracle)))\\n295:           (cst/parse-unit (cst/make-worker profile) [:unit 0]\\n296:                           (:source (first (:entries oracle)))))\\n297:        (:id (first (:entries oracle))))\\n298: \\n299: \\n300: ;; -- the completeness probe ----------------------------------------------------\\n301: \\n302: (require '[yang.python.antlr.repl-probe :as probe])\\n303: \\n304: \\n305: (check \\\"repl-probe's parser is the stand-in (no rule-names: the tracked parser.cljc did not load) with the same grammar id\\\"\\n306:        (and (nil? (resolve 'yang.python.antlr.parser/rule-names))\\n307:             (= grammar-id @(resolve 'yang.python.antlr.parser/grammar-id)))\\n308:        @(resolve 'yang.python.antlr.parser/grammar-id))\\n309: \\n310: \\n311: (doseq [{:keys [text first-failure-calls], answer :probe} (:probe oracle)]\\n312:   (let [calls (volatile! [])\\n313:         ff cst/first-failure\\n314:         jolt-answer (with-redefs [cst/first-failure\\n315:                                   (fn [w s] (let [r (ff w s)] (vswap! calls conj [s r]) r))]\\n316:                       (probe/probe text))\\n317:         ok (and (= answer jolt-answer) (= first-failure-calls @calls))]\\n318:     (when-not ok (swap! diverged conj [:probe text]))\\n319:     (println (if ok \\\"PASS\\\" \\\"FAIL\\\") \\\"probe\\\" (pr-str text)\\n320:              (pr-str (cond-> {:probe answer, :calls first-failure-calls}\\n321:                        (not ok) (assoc :jolt-probe jolt-answer, :jolt-calls @calls))))))\\n322: \\n323: \\n324: (let [rs @results]\\n325:   (println \\\"RECORD totals\\\"\\n326:            (pr-str {:entries (count rs),\\n327:                     :e1 (count (filter :e1 rs)),\\n328:                     :strict-packets-equal (count (filter :strict rs)),\\n329:                     :first-failure-equal (count (filter :first-failure rs)),\\n330:                     :probe-texts (count (:probe oracle)),\\n331:                     :diverged @diverged})))\\n332: \\n333: \\n334: (println \\\"SUMMARY\\\" (if (and (empty? @diverged) (zero? @failed))\\n335:                      \\\"ok\\\"\\n336:                      (str (count @diverged) \\\" diverged, \\\" @failed \\\" failed\\\")))\\n337: \\n338: \\n339: (System/exit (if (and (empty? @diverged) (zero? @failed)) 0 1))\"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"4fee08f7-31bc-48dc-9bbd-75795832bbf0\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Adversarial analysis of the E1 harness:\\n\\nFidelity to the ruling:\\n- Ruling E1: strict comparison of ok-packet fields (:outcome :complete :nodes :root), first-failure, and probe responses + call sequences. Harness: ok packet → full packet equality `(= jvm jolt)` (stronger than the enumerated fields, no problem). Error packet → base fields excluding :yang.cst/errors are strictly compared (including outcome, version, unit, grammar, complete — everything except errors). ✓\\n- Error packet: validate! with source ✓; ≥1 error ✓; :kind and :span of the first error equal ✓; message is a non-empty string ✓; line/column: real tokens equal to JVM, helper-generated ones equal to fixed pins ✓. Helper-generated ones defined as INDENT/DEDENT/EOF/zero-width NEWLINE — the ruling says \\\"INDENT, DEDENT, synthetic NEWLINE, EOF\\\". Harness's helper-token? uses jolt's own problematic-token-type record (from the record's :type), with zero-width NEWLINE detection as `(and (= \\\"NEWLINE\\\" tn) (< b a))` — using the span's a/b from the error record. Matches the ruling.\\n- Exemptions: message body, expecting set, errors from the first onward ✓.\\n\\nIs there any way the harness could pass while the packet is still wrong?\\n- Errors from the first onward are exempted by the ruling — could they hide a wrong error count? Yes. But this is the E1 the ruling acknowledged (consumers only read the first one). The strict-diff RECORD line keeps them visible (3 entries recorded). The ruling states that count is not a consumer concern. OK.\\n- Node comparison on error packets: the ruling says node strictness only for ok packets. The harness exempts nodes on error packets! Wait — ruling E1: \\\"strict: :outcome, :complete, :nodes, :root of ok packets\\\". So nodes on error packets are not subject to strict comparison in the ruling either. The harness compares base fields (dissoc errors) — including :yang.cst/nodes! `(base jvm) vs (base jolt)` — since base only dissocs :yang.cst/errors, nodes are strictly compared even on error packets. Stronger than the ruling. Good (no weakening).\\n- validate! catches exceptions and records ex-data as a violation ✓. If validate! returns without throwing but the packet is invalid… validate! is the shared contract.\\n- Thrown jolt packets are counted as violations [[:thrown ...]] → FAIL-E1 ✓.\\n- ff-ok is a strict comparison ✓.\\n- Pins: helper-token-unpinned is a violation; pins for real-token entries are violations; pins not in the corpus are violations (check :243-245). So the pin set cannot be silently expanded; adding a pin to an entry whose first error is a real token will fail. Can the harness be gamed by pinning everything? No — pin-on-a-real-token will fail. Can a genuine line/column mismatch slip through via a pin? Only if the problematic token is helper-generated, and only if the pin matches jolt's (wrong) value — but the pins equal JVM values (the report states all 9 equal JVM; verifiable from PASS + strict diff records: entries 1, 2, 3's lines are now strictly equal — meaning the pin values equal JVM values). Actually, is pin == JVM enforced? For helper tokens, the harness compares the pin to JOLT's line/column, not JVM's. If the pin had been set to a wrong jolt value different from JVM, E1 would still pass — that is by design the very purpose of per-host pinning (the ruling permits per-host constants). But the ruling says \\\"because the patch gives the C++ helper Java's coordinates, everything now equals JVM\\\" — the report claims equivalence; the RECORD strict-diff shows the remaining diffs are message-only for these entries, corroborating that line/column equal JVM. Honest.\\n- Self-test: 7 mutated packets, each expecting specific violation reasons; `(every? got expect)` — checks that all expected reasons are present. Note: run computes e1-violations on the mutated packet, but `records` is obtained from the actual parse (unmutated) — helper-token? uses the actual record; appropriate. The mutation set covers the checker's branches: validate!+pin-drift, pin-drift, first-line-column, first-span, first-message, no-errors, ok-packet-not-equal. Are these genuine tests of the checker: yes, they mutate real jolt packets and assert that the checker flags them for the right reasons. Not vacuous. One gap: no self-test for :helper-token-unpinned and :pin-on-a-real-token (the pin-discipline branches). Minor — could mention it. The self-test for \\\"INDENT at line 0\\\" expects #{:validate! :helper-pin-drift} and got must contain both — good (also verifies the round-2 helper's value violates the packet contract, corroborating the architect's (d)).\\n\\nHonesty of the pins: 9 pins discovered via the discovery path (r3-discover.log), the reason each entry's first error is a helper token is documented in one line; the NEWLINE first-error entry (malformed-unexpected-newline) has width → real → checked against JVM. Pin values are reasonable (INDENT after a space is line 3 col 2, \\\"\\\\n\\\\n  \\\\n\\\", etc.). Tuned to pass? The pins equal JVM values, and drift fails; discovery was type-driven, not outcome-driven. Honest.\\n\\nPins are in the harness rather than the fixture (the ruling says fixture): deviation disclosed by the author (report line 29), per the brief's instruction. Acceptable? The ruling's intent is that pins are tracked, reviewed, and drift causes failure — the harness is a tracked source file; but the fixture would bind pins to oracle regeneration. The brief explicitly instructed the harness. Flag as an accepted deviation, recommend moving to fixture at J5 conformance (the ruling's J5 item states \\\"jolt pins via conformance/check-error\\\"). Info level.\\n\\n(b) Helper patch: exact-text mechanism, uniqueness enforced, digest re-pinned (65027edf → 2483e344), the first regeneration fails closed (exit 1, r3-gen-cpp-1.log), Java/JS/Dart digests are byte-identical (verified via grep: bb61a463/1c8ac0a2/8ba1f239/33274579; JS 855f47de/4724a9da/37f27bdb/a569e60f; Dart a29f3f8c/e3b11f88/fa002711/e4c4693a — all equal to base). DEDENT guard: `if (lastToken) dedent->setLine(lastToken->getLine());` — Java dereferences unconditionally; the guard prevents null dereference (DEDENT without a preceding token cannot naturally occur; the guard is disclosed in the manifest comment :210-212). Sound and disclosed. commonToken patch: replaces `lastToken ? lastToken->getLine() : 0, ...` with `getLine(), getCharPositionInLine()` — matches Java's lexer-derived coordinates. One question: old-text uniqueness — the old line for commonToken includes `type, text`, which is distinguishable from positionedToken's `type, \\\"\\\"` inserted earlier — and patches apply in order, with apply-patch requiring exactly one match; regeneration exit 0 proves application. ✓\\n\\n(c) Layout v2: JPX1 version 1→2 (:289 o.u32(2)), adds i32 offending-token type (-2 if none) to error records; cst.jolt rejects non-2 layouts (:123), decodes :type (:140). JPT1 round-1 entry unchanged — confirmed by reading: jolt_parser_parse region is untouched (the earlier grep showed only JPX1 modifications; round-1 probe 11 PASS re-run r3-probe-r1.log). Containment unchanged: export entry has the same try/catch, message() noexcept unchanged, fault seam shared. cst.jolt's bounds/topology validation unchanged except for version. Backward compat: nothing outside cst.jolt consumes JPX1 v1 (the round-2 harness used cst.jolt); version bump + strict rejection is the right choice. ✓\\n\\n(d) G1: `(or (System/getProperty \\\"os.name\\\") \\\"\\\")` — nil → \\\"\\\" → linux-x86_64; documented; keeps the load closure small (does not require jolt.socket.native — the load-closure assertion requires only cst+export+packet; adding a native require would break the assertion). Reasonable resolution; untested on an actually-nil host (disclosed). ✓ closed (macOS side), Linux-side test deferred and disclosed.\\n\\n(e) Report-to-log correspondence: r3-parity-final.log should show 106 PASS. Pass count: 51 entries + 40 probes + checks (adapter load 1, oracle 1, pin names 1, self-test 7, injected 3, re-parse 1) = 51+40+14 = 105… plus? Hmm 106. The entry PASS lines output by the entry loop = 51 (all pass), probe 40, standalone checks: 1 (adapter) + 1 (oracle) + 1 (pin) + 7 (self-test) + 3 (injected) + 1 (after injected) = 14. 51+40+14=105. The report says 106 PASS. Off by one — perhaps there's one more check somewhere… \\\"e1 51\\\" RECORD totals. Should v