6-10-20 21:21:25 +07\\n3: Coding-Agent: claude\\n4: Session-ID: 4f6a4fdd-c79b-44eb-97f2-1ce81ad82aa3\\n5: # Findings: J0c round 4 - worker isolation, thread/stack evidence, input policy, leak checks, native hardening\\n6: Role: Compiler & AST / Stream engineer\\n7: Implementers:\\n8: - Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-20 19:45:14 +07 | Status: done (round 4, macOS arm64) | Rationale: owner routing \\\"opus-5.5 for implementation, cmd for review\\\"\\n9: \\n10: **Worktree:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c` (branch `jolt-j0c`, base `522a7930`). Nothing is staged or committed.\\n11: **Logs:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r4-*`.\\n12: **Platform:** macOS arm64 only. Linux x86_64 is NOT RUN; the orchestrator runs it.\\n13: **File scope:** `native/jolt-parser/`, `src/jolt/`, `src/dev/jolt_*`, and two new fixture files beside the round-2 fixtures.\\n14: \\n15: ## Ladder\\n16: \\n17: | step | result | evidence |\\n18: |---|---|---|\\n19: | A. Worker isolation (§7.4) | **DONE**, one audit item for review (A.3) | `r4-workers-final.log`: 8 PASS, SUMMARY ok |\\n20: | B. Thread and stack, collection during a long parse (§7.5, §7.6) | **DONE**: measured, no bound enforced | `r4-stack.log` / `build/j0c/r4/stack-budget.edn`, `r4-gc.log` |\\n21: | C. Input policy | **DONE**; it found and fixed one real defect: the BOM | `r4-parity-final.log`: 132 PASS, SUMMARY ok |\\n22: | D. Leaks and lifetime | **DONE** (LSan is not available on macOS; `leaks` used instead) | `r4-cycles-20000.log`, `r4-sanitize.log` |\\n23: | E. Native hardening | **DONE** (the Linux version script is untested) | `r4-nm.log`, `r4-digests.log`, `r4-probe-r1-release.log` |\\n24: \\n25: **Still green:**\\n26: - `bb smoke:jolt`: SUMMARY ok.\\n27: - round-1 probe: 11 PASS on the test build; on the release build 7 PASS plus a SKIP message for the 4 fault checks.\\n28: - E1 parity: SUMMARY ok, `e1 51`, strict 48/51, first-failure 51/51, probes 40/40, plus the new input section.\\n29: \\n30: ## A. Worker isolation\\n31: \\n32: ### A.1 Mechanism\\n33: \\n34: The design fixes this, and it matches the JVM `cst.clj:43-71`.\\n35: \\n36: **Native side** (`jolt_parser.cpp`):\\n37: - `jolt_parser_worker_new` returns an opaque `Worker`. It owns fresh `std::vector<dfa::DFA>` for the lexer and the parser, built over the generated ATNs' decision states, plus two `PredictionContextCache`s.\\n38: - `jolt_parser_export(worker, …)` installs fresh simulators over the worker's caches before recognition, replacing the generated defaults that point at the static caches:\\n39:   - `lexer.setInterpreter(new LexerATNSimulator(&lexer, *atn, w->lexer_dfa, w->lexer_ctx))`;\\n40:   - the same for the parser.\\n41: - `Recognizer::setInterpreter` deletes the generated default (`runtime/src/Recognizer.cpp:88-91`), and the generated destructors delete the installed one. So the simulators live per call, and the caches live as long as the worker.\\n42: - `jolt_parser_worker_free` disposes the handle.\\n43: \\n44: **Guards:**\\n45: - A tag rejects a NULL or unrecognized handle with −1.\\n46: - A per-worker atomic busy flag refuses a concurrent second call with a new status, **−5**. It is fail-closed, enforcing \\\"a worker is used by one thread at a time\\\" (§7.4).\\n47: \\n48: **jolt side** (`cst.jolt`):\\n49: - `make-worker` holds the handle in an atom.\\n50: - New `close-worker` is idempotent: a `compare-and-set!` frees it once.\\n51: - A closed worker throws \\\"jolt parser worker is closed\\\".\\n52: - `export-records` now takes the worker, and `first-failure` uses it.\\n53: \\n54: **The design gap to settle:** `close-worker` is jolt-only; the other hosts' workers are collected. The parser stage's close/reset (§7.4: \\\"its parser stage owns it and disposes it at close/reset\\\") lives in shared code (`yang.stage`, `yang.python.antlr.parser`, `repl_probe`). That is outside this track's files, so **those callers do not call it yet**. `repl_probe` makes a temporary worker per probe call, and under jolt each one keeps its native caches until the process exits. Wiring disposal into the stage and the probe is J5/integration work. The stand-in parser used by the harness has the same leak.\\n55: \\n56: ### A.2 Tests (`src/dev/jolt_j0c_workers.clj`, 8 PASS)\\n57: \\n58: - **Isolated reference:** each of the 51 corpus sources is parsed with its own fresh worker.\\n59: - **Two workers on ONE thread:**\\n60:   - A runs forwards and B backwards, alternating calls, for 3 passes;\\n61:   - every packet equals the isolated run.\\n62: - **4 workers on 4 threads:**\\n63:   - they start together behind a gate, each does 3 passes (153 parses) in mixed orders;\\n64:   - every packet equals the isolated run.\\n65: - **No static growth.** The generated static caches were counted through the test seam `jolt_parser_static_cache_stats`:\\n66:   - after initialization: lexer DFA 0 states/0 edges/0 ctx; parser DFA 1 state (the precedence-DFA start state the constructor makes)/0 edges/0 ctx;\\n67:   - after all worker parses: **identical**.\\n68: - **Positive control 1:** worker A's own caches filled to lexer DFA 89 states/265 edges, parser DFA 143 states/254 edges, 213 parser ctx entries.\\n69: - **Positive control 2:** one parse through the round-1 `jolt_parser_parse`, which uses the static caches, grew them to lexer 33/64 and parser 57/60/105. So the counters are live.\\n70: - **Busy refusal:** a second call while a long parse runs on the same worker is refused with −5, and the long parse (1,000,002 records) succeeds.\\n71: - **Close:** a closed worker refuses parses, and a second close is a no-op.\\n72: - **Concurrency observed:** a call on another worker runs concurrently on the main thread meanwhile (from `build/j0c/r2/overlap.clj`, a throwaway probe).\\n73: \\n74: ### A.3 Static-state audit (generated C++, helpers, runtime 4.13.2)\\n75: \\n76: **Generated, mutable, per-grammar static (the caches §7.4 names):**\\n77: - `Python3Lexer.cpp:33-34` and `Python3Parser.cpp:29-30`: `decisionToDFA`, `sharedContextCache` inside `Python3{Lexer,Parser}StaticData`.\\n78: - Held in the anonymous-namespace globals `Python3Lexer.cpp:49` and `Python3Parser.cpp:43`, initialized once through `call_once` (`Python3Lexer.cpp:763`).\\n79: - Wired into the default simulators at `Python3Lexer.cpp:623` and `Python3Parser.cpp:667`.\\n80: - `ANTLR4_USE_THREAD_LOCAL_CACHE` is not defined, so these are process-wide, not thread-local.\\n81: - **Replaced per worker (above), and shown not to grow.**\\n82: \\n83: **Generated, immutable after init (shared):**\\n84: - the ATN, `serializedATN`, `ruleNames`, `vocabulary`, `literalNames`, `symbolicNames`.\\n85: \\n86: **Helpers:**\\n87: - `Python3LexerBase` keeps only per-instance state: `tokens`, `indents`, `opened`, `lastToken`.\\n88: - `getIndentationCount` is a static pure function.\\n89: - `onNewLine` builds its `std::regex` per call.\\n90: - `Python3ParserBase` has no state.\\n91: - **No static mutable state.**\\n92: \\n93: **Runtime statics:**\\n94: - `tree/xpath/XPathLexer.cpp:46` (thread_local XPath lexer data), `tree/xpath/XPath.cpp:123`, `tree/ParseTreeWalker.cpp:18`, and `Parser.cpp:47` (`BypassAltsAtnCache`). None of them is reached by `file_input` recognition or by the wrapper; they serve XPath, tree walking and parse-tree patterns.\\n95: - `ATN::_mutex/_stateMutex/_edgeMutex` (`ATN.h:127-129`) are locks only.\\n96: \\n97: **OPEN, for review:** the shared ATN's next-token memo.\\n98: - `ATN::nextTokens(ATNState*)` lazily fills `ATNState::_nextTokenWithinRule` under `ATN::_mutex` (`runtime/src/atn/ATN.cpp:40-49`). It lives in the shared, generated ATN.\\n99: - **It does grow during worker recognition:** 0 → 99 parser ATN states after the corpus. The test records this (RECORD line); it does not assert it.\\n100: - It is a deterministic memo of a pure function of the ATN alone (no input or worker state), set once per state under a lock, and never read before it is set.\\n101: - The JVM shares exactly the same memo: Java's `ATN.nextTokens(ATNState)` caches `s.nextTokenWithinRule`, and `cst.clj` shares the generated ATN across workers. The design cites `cst.clj` as the isolation reference, so I read §7.4 as accepting it and **did not stop A**.\\n102: - If the Architect reads \\\"mutable caches may not [be shared]\\\" literally, there are two options:\\n103:   - (a) fill the memo for every ATN state once, at library initialization (cheap, a few thousand states), which makes it immutable before any recognition;\\n104:   - (b) deserialize a private ATN per worker (memory per worker, no shared ATN state at all).\\n105: - **I chose neither.**\\n106: \\n107: ## B. Thread, stack, collection\\n108: \\n109: **Actual thread configuration,** read on the parsing thread itself through `jolt_parser_thread_info` (`pthread_get_stacksize_np`/`pthread_get_stackaddr_np`/`pthread_main_np`):\\n110: \\n111: | parsing thread | stack bytes | main thread | used at the call |\\n112: |---|---|---|---|\\n113: | jolt's script/main thread (`jolt -M:jolt file.clj`) | 10,027,008 | yes | 9,089 B |\\n114: | a thread jolt starts with `Thread.` | 536,576 | no | 241 B |\\n115: \\n116: - **The `:blocking` parse runs on the calling thread:** the reported stack is the caller's own.\\n117: - **The main thread's stack is RLIMIT_STACK:** this shell's `ulimit -s` is 9792 KiB = 10,027,008 bytes. So the main-thread budget is inherited from whoever launches jolt and is **not a property of the build**.\\n118: - **A jolt `Thread.` gets the macOS default 512 KiB pthread stack:** 536,576 bytes as reported. jolt sets no thread stack size that I could see.\\n119: \\n120: **Nesting budget** (`bb src/dev/jolt_j0c_stack.clj`):\\n121: - Every parse runs in a child jolt process, so a crash kills only the child.\\n122: - Per shape and per thread kind: doubling depths, then 16 steps inside the failing interval, then a FRESH child (cold caches) at the last passing depth through the full jolt path (native, decode, shared exporter).\\n123: - Native-only runs (`native` mode) crash at the same depths as full runs (parens: main passes 8,000 and dies before 16,000 in both modes; a jolt thread passes 500 and dies before 1,000), so **the overflow is the native recursion** (ANTLR's recursive descent and the wrapper's walk), not the jolt side.\\n124: \\n125: Final run: `r4-stack.log`, written to `build/j0c/r4/stack-budget.edn`, exit 0. The time cap is 20 s per parse. \\\"passes\\\" is the last depth that passed in the 16-step refinement; a fresh child (cold caches) then passed it again through the full jolt path.\\n126: \\n127: | shape | main thread (10,027,008 B): passes / dies at / exit / B per level | jolt `Thread.` (536,576 B): passes / dies at / exit / B per level |\\n128: |---|---|---|\\n129: | `(`…`)` parens | 10,752 / 11,264 / 139 SIGSEGV / 890 | 544 / 576 / 132 SIGILL / 931 |\\n130: | `[`…`]` list | 10,752 / 11,264 / 139 / 890 | 544 / 576 / 132 / 931 |\\n131: | `f(`…`)` call | 9,728 / 10,240 / 139 / 979 | 512 / 544 / 132 / 986 |\\n132: | unary `-`…`-1` | **time-limited before any crash:** 512 in 10.3 s, 1,024 in 93.5 s. Time grows about ×6–9 per doubling (cubic-like) | 640 passes (fresh full re-run 20.7 s). The doubling ladder crashed (exit 132) between 512 and 1,024; the refinement stopped at the time cap at 672, not on a crash. Treat this one as time-bound |\\n133: | `not not … 1` | no failure up to 65,536 (the ladder top) | 5,376 / 5,632 / 132 / 95 |\\n134: | `2 ** 2 ** …` | no failure up to 65,536 | 6,656 / 6,912 / 132 / 77 |\\n135: | `lambda: lambda: …` | 55,296 / 57,344 / 139 / 174 | 2,944 / 3,072 / 132 / 174 |\\n136: | `1 if 1 else …` (ternary) | no crash up to 65,536. Time is linear: 32,768 in 14.2 s, and 65,536 parsed fine in 28.5 s but went past the cap, which ended the ladder | 5,376 / 5,632 / 132 / 95 |\\n137: | nested `if` blocks (indentation) | no failure up to 8,192 (that ladder's top; the input grows quadratically) | 1,280 / 1,344 / 132 / 399 |\\n138: \\n139: **Comparison, the JVM oracle on its default main-thread stack** (`clojure -M -e`):\\n140: - parens nesting 256 parses; **512 throws `StackOverflowError`** (catchable on the JVM).\\n141: - unary takes 328 ms / 986 ms / 6,005 ms at 128/256/512, the same super-linear growth, so it is the grammar/ALL(*) cost and not the C++ runtime's.\\n142: - So the C++ main thread nests deeper than the JVM oracle can. Parity beyond the JVM's own limit has no oracle; J5 must decide the bound.\\n143: \\n144: **Failure mode at and above the bracket:**\\n145: - **Main thread:** the process dies with SIGSEGV (exit 139).\\n146:   - This holds for every crashing shape on both thread kinds; the jolt `Thread.` line below likewise.\\n147: - **jolt `Thread.`:** the process dies with SIGILL (exit 132).\\n148: - Either way nothing is reported, there is no exception, and no catch is possible, as §7.6 says. Every crash happened in a child, and the harness survived and recorded it.\\n149: \\n150: **No bound is enforced in `cst.jolt`;** §7.6 assigns enforcement to J5. **Budget policy is not chosen here.** Options for J5:\\n151: - per-thread-kind budgets from the measured `passes` with a safety factor (for example 1/2);\\n152: - run parses on an owned thread created with an explicit large stack, so the budget no longer depends on RLIMIT_STACK or jolt's default;\\n153: - a pre-parse nesting count, with the §7.6 caveat that brackets alone do not bound every shape (unary, `not`, `lambda`, ternary and `**` nest without brackets; see the table);\\n154: - **and a time/resource bound, not only a stack bound.** Prefix unary nesting reaches 93.5 s at depth 1,024, long before any overflow, on the JVM as well. No asynchronous cancellation of a native call exists (§7.6), so bounding the input is the only defence against this input.\\n155: \\n156: **Collection during a long parse** (`src/dev/jolt_j0c_gc.clj`, `r4-gc.log`):\\n157: \\n158: | | value |\\n159: |---|---|\\n160: | setup | one `jolt_parser_export` call (30,000 statements, 28,203,837-byte answer) on thread P, encoded before P starts; `jolt.host/gc-full!` on main 200 ms into the call; unrelated thread U ticks every ~5 ms |\\n161: | `:blocking` | call 0.1 → 35,826 ms; collection 206.8 → 271.1 ms (**completed during the call**); U: 5,237 ticks during the call |\\n162: | plain binding (recorded, not asserted) | collection 203.4 → 349.2 ms, also during the call; U: 4,830 ticks |\\n163: \\n164: - As J0a found, this does not distinguish the two modes on jolt; no claim rests on `:blocking` semantics.\\n165: \\n166: **Observation, not a performance claim:**\\n167: - Native parse time is linear, at about 0.9 ms per statement of `x = (1 + 2) * 3`: 1,000 → 880 ms, 8,000 → 7,728 ms.\\n168: - The JVM path (parse plus export) is about 0.48 ms per statement.\\n169: - Both were measured with the stack run loading the CPU.\\n170: - One cause seen in a profile: the Python3 lexer's start state carries a semantic predicate (`atStartOfInput()`), so DFA state 0 is never cached (`suppressEdge`), and every token runs `LexerATNSimulator::matchATN`. The JVM runtime behaves the same way.\\n171: \\n172: ## C. Input policy\\n173: \\n174: **Tracked fixtures:**\\n175: - corpus: `test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_input_corpus.edn`, 13 sources;\\n176: - oracle: `jolt_j0c_input_oracle.edn`, SHA-256 `954a479379c449247737432ee1c4b6c1ccc66f6d3c7331e45b39ec567658bbf0`. It is written only on the JVM by `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle input` and records each source's code points.\\n177: - The round-2 oracle is untouched: `5aee6d10…`, unchanged, and it regenerates identically.\\n178: \\n179: | case | policy | result |\\n180: |---|---|---|\\n181: | embedded NUL: in a string, in a comment, after a statement, alone, in indentation | parity with the JVM (E1) | 5/5 PASS, all strictly equal |\\n182: | empty, CRLF, lone CR, U+FFFD, U+10FFFF, U+FFFF, DEL | parity (E1) | 7/7 PASS, strictly equal |\\n183: | **BOM at start** `\\\"﻿x = 1\\\\n\\\"` | parity (E1) | **FAILED first, then fixed**: see below. PASS, strictly equal |\\n184: | invalid UTF-8 at the C ABI: lone 0xFF, truncated sequence, overlong `/`, encoded U+D800, above U+10FFFF, 0xFF in a string literal | no JVM equivalent (a JVM String cannot hold one). Fail closed: −2, message copied out, no JPX1 buffer | 6/6 PASS |\\n185: | invalid UTF-8 from jolt | a jolt string cannot carry it: jolt decodes 0xFF to U+FFFD, as the JVM's `new String` does, and its reader refuses `\\\"\\\\uD800\\\"` | observed, not reachable through `cst` |\\n186: | output over 64 MiB (one 70 MiB string token) | −4, no buffer; `cst` throws, so no packet; the same worker parses again | PASS (also in the ASan harness) |\\n187: | invalid arguments: NULL source with a length, NULL out cell, NULL worker, unrecognized worker (zeroed memory) | −1, no buffer | 4/4 PASS |\\n188: \\n189: **BOM defect, found by C and fixed.**\\n190: - **Cause:** `ANTLRInputStream::load` strips a leading UTF-8 BOM (`runtime/src/ANTLRInputStream.cpp:43-48`); the JVM's `CharStreams.fromString` keeps it.\\n191: - **Effect:** jolt parsed `\\\"﻿x = 1\\\\n\\\"` as **ok**, with every index one code point off the source. `validate!` refused it (`token-text-not-source`), and the JVM answers a syntax error. That is a wrong *successful* packet, which C forbids.\\n192: - **Fix:** the wrapper's `SourceStream` (a subclass of `ANTLRInputStream`) decodes with the runtime's own `antlrcpp::Utf8::strictDecode`, throwing the same \\\"illegal byte sequence\\\" on invalid input, and keeps U+FEFF. It is used by both entries.\\n193: - **After the fix:** strictly equal to the JVM, and the 51 E1 entries are unchanged.\\n194: \\n195: **Also hardened:** the 64 MiB bound is now enforced **while the buffer grows** (an `Oversize` marker is thrown and answers −4). Before, the whole answer was built and only then compared with the bound.\\n196: \\n197: **Not covered:** there is no input-size bound. Only the output is bounded; the input is limited by memory and by the B recursion limits (J5).\\n198: \\n199: ## D. Leaks and lifetime\\n200: \\n201: **Cycles from jolt** (`src/dev/jolt_j0c_cycles.clj`; heap = `jolt_parser_heap_in_use`, malloc's bytes in use for the whole process; a full jolt collection before each sample):\\n202: \\n203: | run | start | end | growth | shape |\\n204: |---|---|---|---|---|\\n205: | 20,000 `cst/parse-unit` cycles over the 51 sources (+ 800 worker make/close) | 1,304,240 | 1,416,912 | +112,672 B | +103 KB in the first 1,000, then flat within 1.39–1.42 MB through 20,000 |\\n206: | 20,000 bare `jolt_parser_export`/`jolt_parser_free` cycles | 1,416,944 | 1,531,392 | +114,448 B | rises to ~1.53 MB by cycle 3,000, then flat |\\n207: \\n208: The 5,000-cycle run (`r4-cycles.log`) agrees.\\n209: \\n210: **AddressSanitizer + UBSan** (`bb src/dev/jolt_j0c_sanitize.clj 50`):\\n211: - **Builds:** the pinned runtime (the archive is checked against its SHA-256) is rebuilt with `-fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all`. The standalone harness `native/jolt-parser/sanitize/harness.cpp` is linked with the wrapper and the generated parser; it is never loaded into jolt.\\n212: - **Each of the 50 cycles:**\\n213:   - worker new;\\n214:   - exports: ok, syntax error, lexer error, BOM, NUL, invalid UTF-8 (−2), empty;\\n215:   - round-1 parse: ok and syntax error;\\n216:   - the three fault modes on both entries;\\n217:   - every stats/thread/heap seam;\\n218:   - worker free.\\n219: - **Once:** the invalid arguments, the 70 MiB oversize answer (−4), then a successful re-parse, and `worker_free(NULL)`.\\n220: - **Result: exit 0, `HARNESS ok`, no ASan or UBSan report.**\\n221: \\n222: **Leaks:**\\n223: - macOS Apple clang has no LeakSanitizer, so the unsanitized build of the same harness ran under `leaks --atExit` (50 cycles): **\\\"0 leaks for 0 total leaked bytes\\\"**, exit 0.\\n224: - On Linux the driver sets `detect_leaks=1` for the ASan run (unrun here).\\n225: \\n226: **Not done:** TSan (the busy guard and the 4-thread run were exercised under ASan's process only, not a race detector).\\n227: \\n228: ## E. Native hardening\\n229: \\n230: - **Exported-symbols list:**\\n231:   - `native/jolt-parser/exports.darwin` (`_jolt_parser_*`, via `-exported_symbols_list`) and `exports.linux` (a version script, `jolt_parser_*` global, the rest local), selected in CMake.\\n232:   - Test build: **12 exports**. Release build: **7**: `export`, `export_layout_version`, `free`, `layout_version`, `parse`, `worker_free`, `worker_new`. Before, there were 4 entries plus about 1,556 `antlr4::` symbols (`r4-nm.log`).\\n233:   - **The Linux version script is unbuilt and untested.**\\n234: - **Test seams behind a CMake option:**\\n235:   - `option(JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS … OFF)` gates `jolt_parser_inject_fault`, the fault branches themselves (`maybe_fault`, `fail_handler_malloc`), and the new stats/thread/heap seams.\\n236:   - `bb build:jolt-native` now builds both variants: `jolt-parser/` (seams ON, what the probes and harnesses load) and `jolt-parser-release/` (CMake default, OFF). It records both, with their exported symbol lists, in `build-record.edn`.\\n237:   - The round-1 probe and the parity harness look the seam up (`ffi/find-symbol`). They print `SKIP … built without JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS` when it is absent; the probe shows this against the release build through `JOLT_PARSER_PROBE_LIB`.\\n238: - **Nonallocating failure paths are kept:** `message()` is unchanged apart from `fail_handler_malloc()`, and `Oversize` and the busy refusal allocate nothing.\\n239: - **Export layout version entry** (cmd G2/H3): new `jolt_parser_export_layout_version()` answers 2. `cst.jolt` checks it at load and refuses a library that writes another layout. The buffer still carries its version, and the decoder still checks that too.\\n240: - **dylibs:** test `2913f3ed9815f68041ca99fd9324eb2d0043bf761396640d0bb043c437d5413e`, release `9361c17641c57139dfab61d59b0e80ef9c7071010a7282f79ee362b3c973c9fd`. Both depend only on `libc++` and `libSystem`.\\n241: - **ABI change:** `jolt_parser_export` now takes the worker first. The JPX1 layout itself is unchanged (version 2).\\n242: \\n243: ## Commands (in the worktree) and exit codes\\n244: \\n245: Long runs: the tool moves any foreground command past 600 s to the background and stops it 10 minutes later. My first full stack run (part 1: parens, list, call), a `unary not` batch and two sanitize runs were stopped that way. The final full stack run and the 50-cycle sanitize run were then launched with `run_in_background` and a long timeout, and I waited for their completion notifications. No other command ran in the background.\\n246: \\n247: | command | exit | log |\\n248: |---|---|---|\\n249: | `bb build:jolt-native` (first: macro clash, Mach `TRUE/FALSE` vs generated token names, so the includes were moved after the generated headers; later builds after the BOM fix) | 1, then 0, 0 | `r4-build.log` |\\n250: | `jolt … src/dev/jolt_j0c_workers.clj` (the first runs failed the busy check on a test race; the test was fixed) | 1, 1, then 0 | `r4-workers-final.log` |\\n251: | `bb src/dev/jolt_j0c_stack.clj` (final, all shapes, with the 20 s per-parse cap added after unary took about 104 s at 1,024 and a run had to be stopped) | 0 | `r4-stack.log`, `build/j0c/r4/stack-budget.edn` |\\n252: | `jolt … src/dev/jolt_j0c_gc.clj` | 0 | `r4-gc.log` |\\n253: | `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle input`; `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle` (round-2 oracle regenerated, SHA-256 unchanged) | 0, 0 | `r4-input-oracle.log`, `r4-oracle-rerun.log` |\\n254: | parity harness (`jolt -Srepro -Sdeps '{:aliases {:j0c-probe-stub {:extra-paths [\\\"src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub\\\"]}}}' -M:jolt:j0c-probe-stub src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj`). Intermediate runs: an NPE in the E1 checker on the BOM entry (fixed: nil-safe); `(ffi/null)` misuse (fixed). Final: 132 PASS | 1, 1, then 0 | `r4-parity-final.log` |\\n255: | `jolt … src/dev/jolt_j0c_cycles.clj 5000` and `… 20000` | 0, 0 | `r4-cycles.log`, `r4-cycles-20000.log` |\\n256: | `bb src/dev/jolt_j0c_sanitize.clj 50` (earlier: a missing `support/Utf8.h` include, exit 1; a 200-cycle run with a 200,000-statement oversize case was stopped as too slow under ASan, and the case was replaced by one large string token) | 0 | `r4-sanitize.log` |\\n257: | `jolt -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj` (test build; then with `JOLT_PARSER_PROBE_LIB=…/jolt-parser-release/…`) | 0 (11 PASS), 0 (7 PASS + SKIP) | `r4-probe-r1.log`, `r4-probe-r1-release.log` |\\n258: | `bb smoke:jolt` | 0 | `r4-smoke.log` |\\n259: | `mise exec -- cljstyle check` on every changed Clojure/EDN file | 0 | n/a |\\n260: | `clojure -M:kondo --lint` on the dev scripts | known classes only: `defcfn` names unresolved, `cst/*` resolved against the JVM `cst.clj`, a stale `validate!` arity, and the pre-existing `run!` warning in `jolt_native.clj` | n/a |\\n261: \\n262: ## Files\\n263: \\n264: **Modified:**\\n265: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp`: workers, the −5 busy guard, `SourceStream` (BOM), the bound enforced while growing, `TEST_SEAMS` gating, the stats/thread/heap seams, the export layout version entry.\\n266: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/CMakeLists.txt`: the `JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS` option, the exported-symbols list / version script.\\n267: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt`: native worker handle, `close-worker`, worker-taking `export-records`, the layout version check at load.\\n268: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_native.clj`: builds the test and release variants, records their exported symbols.\\n269: - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj`: the fault checks skip without the seam; `JOLT_PAR