{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'defn.*(lane-tests|test|ns|serialized)|serialized-pairs|port|:cljs|:cljd|find-ns|read-ns' src/dev/affected.clj | head -100; nl -ba docs/agents/build-n-test.md | sed -n '350,430p'; nl -ba orchestrator-logs/1793292851-yang-python-retake.md | sed -n '200,275p'\"","aggregated_output":"7:   read for its first ns form, once per host feature (:clj :cljs :cljd),\n42:(def lanes [:clj :cljs :cljd])\n85:(defn ns-name-of\n128:(defn- first-ns-form\n139:(defn ns-info\n141:   union over the :clj :cljs and :cljd readings and a reading no branch\n155:(defn path->ns\n162:(defn convention-test-paths\n170:(defn mentions?\n301:(defn- ns-graph\n458:    :cljs (#{\"cljs\" \"cljc\"} ext)\n460:    :cljd (re-find #\"_test\\.clj[cd]$\" path)))\n463:(defn lane-tests\n474:(defn ns-regexp\n494:  (if (= :cljd lane)\n527:(defn serialized-pairs\n532:  (if (and (seq (:clj selected)) (seq (:cljd selected))\n535:    #{[:clj :cljd]}\n683:     :cljs [\"clj\" \"-M:cljs\" \"-m\" \"shadow.cljs.devtools.cli\" \"compile\" \"test\"\n685:     :cljd [\"bb\" \"src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\" \"--only\" (str/join \",\" nses)])))\n700:               (and (not slow?) (= :cljs lane)) (dissoc \"DATOM_SLOW_TESTS\"))\n801:                serial (serialized-pairs index run)]\n857:                serial (serialized-pairs index run)]\n   350\tunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\n   351\tA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n   352\t\n   353\t**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\n   354\trun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\n   355\totherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n   356\t\n   357\t```sh\n   358\tNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n   359\t# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n   360\t#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n   361\t# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n   362\t# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n   363\t```\n   364\t\n   365\t**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\n   366\tshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\n   367\ttests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\n   368\tJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n   369\t\n   370\t```sh\n   371\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n   372\t# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\n   373\tset -u\n   374\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n   375\tcd \"$1\" || exit 2\n   376\tPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\n   377\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n   378\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n   379\tOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n   380\tts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\n   381\techo \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   382\tnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   383\tbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   384\tbb gen:antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   385\tSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n   386\t: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n   387\tfor s in $SUBS; do\n   388\t  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n   389\t  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n   390\t  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\n   391\tdone\n   392\t# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\n   393\tfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\n   394\tsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\n   395\tcomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\n   396\techo \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   397\trun_shard() {\n   398\t  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n   399\t  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n   400\t  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n   401\t  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   402\t}\n   403\texport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\n   404\tprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\n   405\techo \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   406\t```\n   407\t\n   408\t**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n   409\t\n   410\t```sh\n   411\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n   412\t# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\n   413\tset -u\n   414\texport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\n   415\tcd \"$1\" || exit 2\n   416\tPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\n   417\tmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\n   418\teval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n   419\tOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\n   420\trun_ns() {\n   421\t  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n   422\t  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n   423\t  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   424\t}\n   425\texport -f run_ns; export OUT\n   426\techo \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   427\txargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\n   428\techo \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n   429\t```\n   430\t\n   200\t  the worktree). NOTE: the S1-era briefs and findings (1793294…-1793340…: the ownership map, the\n   201\t  inventory findings, the S1/S3 briefs, the pins/resume/expectations rounds, the S1 gate) were\n   202\t  absent from collab/ at archive time — likely swept by an interim bulk pass; their substance lives\n   203\t  in this log, the landed commits, and the gate artifacts that survived. collab/ is now empty.\n   204\tThe final handoff: the yang.python seat's 8.10 integration is DONE; the next seat (or this one, on\n   205\t  return) owns the 8.10.11 conformance evidence (bidirectional/higher-order cross-language fixtures,\n   206\t  bundled/linked equivalence — the entry fixture is the base), the worker-isolation debt row, and\n   207\t  monitoring the nightly. The standing rules: the two-tier testing policy, the dao.stream merge\n   208\t  precondition, the laptop-unload rule, the shared-checkout staging rule (nobody's staging area),\n   209\t  the slow-bodies rule for slow-guarded surfaces. Prior session log: docs/orchestrator-log.md (the\n   210\t  pre-handoff era) + this file. The tree: origin/master at efc99cc4.\n   211\t## 2026-10-31 12:5x +07 — the nightly rescheduled to 3am VIETNAM time (the owner: shiva is in Philadelphia)\n   212\tThe owner's correction: shiva sits in Philadelphia (EDT), so the 3am crontab fired at shiva's local\n   213\t  3am — the wrong window (it would also drift an hour when US DST ends). Reinstalled: the crontab\n   214\t  now fires HOURLY and ~/ci/nightly-full.sh carries a guard that runs the suite only when\n   215\t  TZ=Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh date +%H == 03 — the run always lands in the Vietnam 3am hour, immune to US\n   216\t  DST shifts forever. The first run: tonight at 03:00 ICT (in ~14 hours from this entry; the\n   217\t  previous 3am-EDT window had not fired yet, so no nightly has run at all — debut tonight). The\n   218\t  cron log: ~/ci/nightly-cron.log; per-stamp summaries: ~/ci/nightly-results/. Cost of the\n   219\t  DST-proof form: the script is invoked 24x/day and exits in ~1s for 23 of them.\n   220\t\n   221\t## 2026-10-31 03:3x +07 — the nightly is now MULTI-CORE (the owner caught the single-core problem)\n   222\tOwner, verbatim: \"it doesn't look like the tests are utilizing multiple cores\" — confirmed: both\n   223\t  hosts ran the monolithic clojure -M:test at ~114% CPU (one core; load ~1.8 on 24/48 cores, the\n   224\t  machines 88-97% idle). Fix: the nightly's JVM lane now uses the jolt seat's proven\n   225\t  ~/ci/shard-jvm.sh (subsystem shards + an uncovered catch-all, verified coverage 276/316 + 40\n   226\t  caught) — 8 parallel on shiva, 12 on jupiter. Live result on shiva: 6 shards done in ~90 seconds\n   227\t  (dao.data 159,000 assertions in 26s), load 19.34. Both hosts' full runs restarted sharded for the\n   228\t  owner's shiva-vs-jupiter comparison (the jupiter copy needed shard-jvm.sh copied over — my\n   229\t  nightly patch had referenced a script that only existed on jupiter; owned and fixed). The cljs\n   230\t  and cljd lanes stay serial for now (the JVM lane dominated the runtime); the shadow-cljs compile\n   231\t  itself parallelizes (526% CPU observed). The nightly script's NOW=1 bypass enables on-demand\n   232\t  full runs outside the 3am window (the owner's \"run the full test now\").\n   233\t\n   234\t## 2026-10-31 09:4x +07 — the nightly-red round routed to muse (the owner's pick); the invocation quirks\n   235\tThe peer brief adopted (collab/1793456746000-orchestrator-yangpython-nightly-red.prompt.md): the\n   236\t  nightly's yang.python shard red on BOTH hosts — 3 failures, same on each. MY adjudication: the\n   237\t  dao.jing guard is ancient correct behavior (H1, 0aa710e7, Sept 23 — not a regression); the\n   238\t  defects are yang.python's stale fixtures/expectations vs the S3 emission changes: (1) the\n   239\t  dht_import fixture's :test/address placeholder now gets READ by the publication path (the\n   240\t  call-address derivation), (2) linked_prelude's module-uast expectations predate the yang.call\n   241\t  emission growing the module shape. Fix routed to muse (the owner's pick) in the nightr worktree\n   242\t  (detached at master, provisioned). MUSE INVOCATION QUIRKS (four failed attempts; for the\n   243\t  delegate-invocation-reference): no -p flag (use --prompt-file or an inline PROMPT, never both);\n   244\t  --worktree-existing must differ from --workspace (pass --workspace alone when working in place);\n   245\t  --trust-workspace required for a fresh worktree (else the run exits after warnings);\n   246\t  muse exec is the headless form; sessions log under ~/.local/share/muse/sessions/YYYY/MM/DD/.\n   247\tThe round's brief: collab/1793465000000-engineer-nightly-red-fix.prompt.md (the G0→G1→tri-host\n   248\t  cycle, the shiva handoff for the heavy lanes, the branch nightly-red-fix).\n   249\tNext: muse's report → my verification (the shiva lanes for the legs it cannot reach) → the\n   250\t  independent review (a different family than muse — codex or kimi per routing) → the landing →\n   251\t  the findings file + the nightly hygiene line (shiva keeps per-shard stdout) back to the owner.\n   252\t\n   253\t## 2026-10-31 10:5x +07 — the nightly-red round LANDED (af506f77); the peer brief's deliverables\n   254\tThe chain: the peer brief adopted → the adjudication (dao.jing correct; the fixtures stale) → the\n   255\t  fix routed to muse per the owner (quota-walled, recorded in routing-status; t